<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · Protest and freedom · 1844 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">A Nation Once Again</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>An Irish song of 1844 hoping that Ireland would one day be a free nation again; for a long time an unofficial Irish anthem.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Thomas Osborne Davis</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>James J. Johnson (the tune of 1887, most often sung today); earlier tunes by an anonymous composer, Thomas Sherlock (1881) and Edward Comerford (1886)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Irish nationalists; the Young Ireland movement</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Ireland</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>first published 13 July 1844; Johnson's tune 1887</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem · Protest and freedom</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (lyrics by Thomas Osborne Davis (d. 1845); the tune most sung, by James J. Johnson, published 1887)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>Thomas Osborne Davis, a Protestant nationalist from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-cork">County Cork</a>, was one of the three co-founders of Young Ireland, the movement that sought Ireland's independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. He held a Romantic idea of Irish identity, shaped by Johann Gottfried von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-gottfried-herder">Herder</a>: Ireland was a spiritual reality grounded in historic culture, and anyone who took it as their homeland, whatever their religion, was Irish. He believed songs could stir the Irish more than speeches and set out to teach the people the songs of their country. &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-nation-once-again">A Nation Once Again</a>&quot; first appeared in Young Ireland's newspaper The Nation, which Davis co-founded and edited, on 13 July 1844, and quickly became a rallying call for the growing nationalist movement.</p><p>The words found their lasting music only slowly. Davis took his melody from Mozart's Clarinet Concerto; the tune by an anonymous composer printed with the text in the collection The Spirit of the Nation proved too complex to catch on. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> journalist Thomas Sherlock, who had Fenian sympathies, wrote a new tune in 1881, and Edward Comerford of Dundalk, son of a Young Irelander, another, first performed at a charity concert in Dundalk Town Hall in the week before 6 March 1886 and published that June; Comerford changed &quot;boyhood&quot; to &quot;childhood&quot; in the first line so that women could sing it too, but no recording of his version is known. The tune that lasted was written by the Dubliner James J. Johnson in 1887, who also composed the music for &quot;God Save Ireland&quot;. It was an immediate success and is the version most often performed today.</p><p>From the 1880s the song became an unofficial anthem of nationalist Ireland. It is counted among the Irish rebel songs and has been classed both as a war song and, conversely, as an anti-war song. John McCormack recorded it in 1906, and it has since been recorded by Our Lady's Choral Society, the Clancy Brothers, the Dubliners, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theobald-wolfe-tone">Wolfe Tones</a> and many céilí bands. On 8 December 1941 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> used its title in a telegram to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eamon-de-valera">Éamon de Valera</a>, pressing Ireland to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>; some read it as an offer of Irish unity, though the British government said the next day that no deal over partition was meant. De Valera did not reply, and Ireland stayed neutral throughout the war. In 2002, after an organised e-mail campaign, the Wolfe Tones' recording was voted the world's most popular song in a BBC World Service listeners' poll.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>The singer recalls learning as a boy of the ancient fighters for freedom, the Spartans at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatii-and-curiatii">Horatii</a>, and dreams of the day when Ireland will be free. He holds that only righteous, upright people can win that freedom and hopes to make himself worthy of the task. Its mood is hopeful and resolute.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When boyhood's fire was in my blood</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>I read of ancient freemen,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For Greece and Rome who bravely stood,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Three hundred men and three men;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And then I prayed I yet might see</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Our fetters rent in twain,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And Ireland, long a province, be</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A Nation once again!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A Nation once again,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A Nation once again,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And Ireland, long a province, be</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A Nation once again!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And from that time, through wildest woe,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>That hope has shone a far light,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Nor could love's brightest summer glow</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Outshine that solemn starlight;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>It seemed to watch above my head</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In forum, field and fane,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Its angel voice sang round my bed,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A Nation once again!</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 44 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A Nation once again,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A Nation once again,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And Ireland, long a province, be</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A Nation once again!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>It whisper'd too, that freedom's ark</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And service high and holy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Would be profaned by feelings dark</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And passions vain or lowly;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For, Freedom comes from God's right hand,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And needs a Godly train;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And righteous men must make our land</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A Nation once again!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A Nation once again,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A Nation once again,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And Ireland, long a province, be</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A Nation once again!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>So, as I grew from boy to man,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>I bent me to that bidding</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>My spirit of each selfish plan</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And cruel passion ridding;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For, thus I hoped some day to aid,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Oh, can such hope be vain?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When my dear country shall be made</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A Nation once again!</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Thomas Osborne Davis (1844). Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Nation_Once_Again" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
